    "case_id": "amb1-case04-research-tool-release",
    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 5 USER]\ne039\tWe learned today that installation is easier than we thought for people using the common package manager, but one private lab had a staff engineer quietly solving problems for everyone.\ne040\tTheir researchers reported a smooth experience because they never saw the setup work.\ne041\tThat makes our private evidence look weaker.\ne042\tOn the other hand, a volunteer wrote a new installer over the weekend and wants real users before polishing it.\ne043\tI cannot tell whether this argues for release or for another round of testing.\n[Turn 5 ASSISTANT]\ne044\tIt argues against treating the private labs as representative, but it does not choose the next step by itself.\ne045\tThe new installer could change the support burden, and public use could test it under real conditions.\ne046\tA bounded installation test with people who lack staff engineering might answer that question faster than either a full beta or more internal polishing.\ne047\tStill, if every uncertainty becomes a separate pre-release study, you recreate the endless preview problem you already identified.\n[Turn 6 USER]\ne048\tThe team could support a public beta for eight weeks if we rotate office hours, but the senior maintainer will be on leave during the last three weeks.\ne049\tPriya says that is fine because it forces knowledge sharing.\ne050\tHe says it turns his absence into an experiment other people did not choose.\ne051\tWe can move the date, but then we miss a workshop where many potential users will be together.\ne052\tThe workshop is a real learning opportunity, not only publicity.\n[Turn 6 ASSISTANT]\ne053\tLaunching into a known reduction in experienced support is a real risk, and calling it knowledge sharing does not assign the work.\ne054\tThe workshop also offers unusually concentrated feedback.\ne055\tOne compromise is to use the workshop as an announced hands-on preview and schedule the public beta after the maintainer returns.\ne056\tThat preserves some learning while delaying open access, though contributors may reasonably see it as another preview and workshop participants may still form expectations.",
